1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,318
Cardiac arrest is an electrical failure of

2
00:00:06,318 --> 00:00:08,477
the heart, and the first minutes belong to

3
00:00:08,477 --> 00:00:10,348
the person nearest the patient.

4
00:00:11,182 --> 00:00:13,330
This lesson sets out the problem and the

5
00:00:13,330 --> 00:00:15,598
chain of survival that answers it.

6
00:00:18,233 --> 00:00:20,074
Cardiac arrest occurs when the heart

7
00:00:20,074 --> 00:00:21,972
malfunctions and stops beating

8
00:00:21,972 --> 00:00:25,731
unexpectedly. It is an electrical problem.

9
00:00:26,573 --> 00:00:28,959
Each normal beat starts as an impulse that

10
00:00:28,959 --> 00:00:30,637
spreads through the heart muscle.

11
00:00:31,475 --> 00:00:33,759
The atria contract first, then the

12
00:00:33,759 --> 00:00:37,243
ventricles. In ventricular fibrillation,

13
00:00:37,243 --> 00:00:39,822
muscle fibres depolarise out of step and

14
00:00:39,822 --> 00:00:41,481
the ventricles quiver instead of

15
00:00:41,481 --> 00:00:45,181
contracting. No blood is ejected, and the

16
00:00:45,181 --> 00:00:47,109
pulse is lost within seconds.

17
00:00:47,957 --> 00:00:50,241
Each year in the United States, about

18
00:00:50,241 --> 00:00:53,116
350,000 people have an out-of-hospital

19
00:00:53,116 --> 00:00:56,418
cardiac arrest, and about 90% of them die.

20
00:00:59,067 --> 00:01:01,562
A heart attack is a circulation problem;

21
00:01:01,578 --> 00:01:04,006
cardiac arrest is an electrical one.

22
00:01:04,833 --> 00:01:07,401
In a heart attack, a coronary artery is

23
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:09,481
blocked and part of the heart muscle is

24
00:01:09,481 --> 00:01:10,613
starved of blood.

25
00:01:11,463 --> 00:01:14,077
In cardiac arrest, the heart stops beating

26
00:01:14,077 --> 00:01:17,554
unexpectedly. The heart attack patient is

27
00:01:17,554 --> 00:01:20,163
usually conscious, often with chest pain.

28
00:01:21,003 --> 00:01:23,571
The arrest patient is unresponsive, with

29
00:01:23,571 --> 00:01:26,333
no pulse and no normal breathing, because

30
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:28,571
without cerebral blood flow consciousness

31
00:01:28,571 --> 00:01:30,260
is lost within seconds.

32
00:01:31,113 --> 00:01:33,249
Occasional gasping, called agonal

33
00:01:33,249 --> 00:01:35,483
breathing, is not normal breathing.

34
00:01:36,327 --> 00:01:38,891
A heart attack can trigger cardiac arrest.

35
00:01:41,533 --> 00:01:43,931
In arrest there is no effective cardiac

36
00:01:43,931 --> 00:01:46,181
output, so chest compressions generate

37
00:01:46,181 --> 00:01:49,203
blood flow. They squeeze the heart between

38
00:01:49,203 --> 00:01:50,999
the sternum and the spine and raise

39
00:01:50,999 --> 00:01:52,403
pressure in the chest.

40
00:01:53,233 --> 00:01:55,438
That produces blood flow to the brain and

41
00:01:55,438 --> 00:01:56,535
to the heart muscle.

42
00:01:57,367 --> 00:01:59,322
Every minute without compressions or

43
00:01:59,322 --> 00:02:01,208
defibrillation lowers the chance of

44
00:02:01,208 --> 00:02:04,574
survival. The compressions continue until

45
00:02:04,574 --> 00:02:05,989
the heart is restarted.

46
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:10,914
Four rhythms cause cardiac arrest.

47
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:14,177
Ventricular fibrillation is shockable:

48
00:02:14,193 --> 00:02:16,455
a shock depolarises the myocardium at

49
00:02:16,455 --> 00:02:18,795
once, so the heart's own pacemaker can

50
00:02:18,795 --> 00:02:20,700
restore an organised rhythm.

51
00:02:21,548 --> 00:02:24,037
Pulseless ventricular tachycardia is also

52
00:02:24,037 --> 00:02:26,605
shockable; the ventricles beat so fast

53
00:02:26,605 --> 00:02:29,112
they cannot fill, so there is no pulse.

54
00:02:29,954 --> 00:02:32,272
Pulseless electrical activity is not

55
00:02:32,272 --> 00:02:34,704
shockable, because the rhythm is already

56
00:02:34,704 --> 00:02:38,248
organised. Asystole is not shockable

57
00:02:38,248 --> 00:02:40,646
either, because there is no electrical

58
00:02:40,646 --> 00:02:42,584
activity for a shock to help.

59
00:02:43,430 --> 00:02:45,453
Both are treated with cardiopulmonary

60
00:02:45,453 --> 00:02:47,146
resuscitation and the search for

61
00:02:47,146 --> 00:02:48,471
reversible causes.

62
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:53,543
Survival to hospital discharge after

63
00:02:53,543 --> 00:02:55,918
out-of-hospital arrest is approximately

64
00:02:55,918 --> 00:03:01,541
10.5%. It is 21.1% in a public place and

65
00:03:01,541 --> 00:03:05,971
8.9% at home. In hospital, survival is

66
00:03:05,971 --> 00:03:11,096
approximately 23.6% in adults and 45.2% in

67
00:03:11,096 --> 00:03:14,497
children. Arrests in public are more often

68
00:03:14,497 --> 00:03:16,224
witnessed and receive bystander

69
00:03:16,224 --> 00:03:18,292
cardiopulmonary resuscitation, and

70
00:03:18,292 --> 00:03:20,554
in-hospital arrests are usually witnessed

71
00:03:20,554 --> 00:03:21,913
with help close by.

72
00:03:22,756 --> 00:03:25,711
Yet only about 41% of adults with

73
00:03:25,711 --> 00:03:27,983
out-of-hospital arrest receive bystander

74
00:03:27,983 --> 00:03:30,097
cardiopulmonary resuscitation before

75
00:03:30,097 --> 00:03:32,433
emergency medical services arrive.

76
00:03:33,274 --> 00:03:35,263
Cardiopulmonary resuscitation given

77
00:03:35,263 --> 00:03:37,433
immediately can double or even triple the

78
00:03:37,433 --> 00:03:38,747
chance of survival.

79
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:44,218
The 2025 chain begins with early

80
00:03:44,218 --> 00:03:46,725
recognition and emergency activation.

81
00:03:47,598 --> 00:03:49,553
The guidelines present one Chain of

82
00:03:49,553 --> 00:03:51,253
Survival with six links.

83
00:03:52,092 --> 00:03:54,410
Second, high-quality cardiopulmonary

84
00:03:54,410 --> 00:03:57,947
resuscitation. Third, defibrillation,

85
00:03:57,947 --> 00:04:00,015
which remains a cornerstone of cardiac

86
00:04:00,015 --> 00:04:03,016
arrest care. Fourth, advanced

87
00:04:03,016 --> 00:04:06,767
resuscitation. Fifth, post–cardiac arrest

88
00:04:06,767 --> 00:04:10,702
care. Sixth, recovery and survivorship.

89
00:04:11,538 --> 00:04:13,220
Previously there were four separate

90
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:16,083
chains, adult and paediatric, in hospital

91
00:04:16,083 --> 00:04:17,477
and out of hospital.

92
00:04:18,312 --> 00:04:20,880
Now a unified chain serves adults and

93
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:23,864
children. Its symbol now includes the

94
00:04:23,864 --> 00:04:26,148
lungs alongside chest compressions,

95
00:04:26,148 --> 00:04:28,421
because breathing matters, especially in

96
00:04:28,421 --> 00:04:30,734
children and in opioid-related arrest.

97
00:04:31,572 --> 00:04:33,765
The details of each link vary between

98
00:04:33,765 --> 00:04:36,011
settings, but the chain is the same.

99
00:04:38,667 --> 00:04:43,466
Much does not change The compression rate

100
00:04:43,466 --> 00:04:46,534
is still 100 to 120 per minute, and the

101
00:04:46,534 --> 00:04:48,239
ratio for single-rescuer adult

102
00:04:48,239 --> 00:04:50,750
cardiopulmonary resuscitation is still 30

103
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:53,863
to 2. The reversible causes and the

104
00:04:53,863 --> 00:04:55,885
overall shape of the algorithms still

105
00:04:55,885 --> 00:04:59,078
hold. Terminology does change.

106
00:04:59,907 --> 00:05:02,766
The term rescue breaths is no longer used.

107
00:05:03,609 --> 00:05:05,825
Breaths means assisted breathing, and

108
00:05:05,825 --> 00:05:07,814
ventilation is reserved for mechanical

109
00:05:07,814 --> 00:05:09,275
respiratory support.

110
00:05:11,933 --> 00:05:14,297
Cardiac arrest is an electrical failure,

111
00:05:14,297 --> 00:05:16,308
and the actions of the nearest person in

112
00:05:16,308 --> 00:05:18,206
the first three minutes matter more than

113
00:05:18,206 --> 00:05:20,206
everything done by everyone else

114
00:05:20,206 --> 00:05:23,710
afterwards. This is a knowledge course; it

115
00:05:23,710 --> 00:05:26,028
does not replace a hands-on basic life

116
00:05:26,028 --> 00:05:28,482
support or advanced cardiovascular life

117
00:05:28,482 --> 00:05:30,755
support provider course with manikin-based

118
00:05:30,755 --> 00:05:32,001
skills assessment.

119
00:05:34,633 --> 00:05:36,401
Keep learning with MedSkills.

